# Slide 4 — Technical Architecture

### “How We Built It”

“From a technical perspective, SafeFlow is designed as a modular recovery framework built around several core layers.

The first layer is the frontend workflow UI, which represents the live workflow experience itself.

This is where users interact with forms, validation logic, and workflow progression.

The second layer is the failure detection engine.

This layer monitors workflow behavior in real time and detects issues such as validation mismatches, malformed state, or failed API interactions.

Once a failure is detected, the workflow context is passed into the AI resolution engine.

This layer integrates with an LLM service to analyze failure patterns and generate contextual recovery recommendations.

However, the most important architectural component is the sandbox state manager.

This layer clones the active workflow session into an isolated frontend state context.

By separating sandbox state from production state, users can safely test fixes independently without affecting the live workflow.

The validation engine then replays validation logic safely inside the sandbox environment.

Once recovery succeeds, the promotion layer allows validated changes to be applied back into the live workflow.

Architecturally, this creates a reusable recovery framework that could potentially support many different enterprise workflows beyond our demo scenario.”

